`]})]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Real momentum is dominated by a few directions",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The measured matrix is the momentum buffer of the first MLP matrix (",e.jsx(s,{math:"1{,}536 \\times 512",display:!1,trail:")"})," in block 3 of the course's Week 5 model, after 4,000 steps of training with Muon. Its largest singular value is 29 times its median one."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["In general an ",e.jsx(s,{math:"m \\times n",display:!1})," matrix has ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\min(m, n)",display:!1})," singular values, however many entries it holds. AdamW treats this matrix as ",e.jsx(s,{math:"1{,}536 \\times 512 = 786{,}432",display:!1})," separate coordinates, while Muon works with its 512 singular directions."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The ten largest of the 512 directions carry 49% of the momentum's energy, the sum of its squared singular values. In the Gaussian matrix they carry 4.6%. This is what ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"effectively low-rank"})," means here: a few directions carry a large share of the matrix (here about half its energy) and the others are small but not zero."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The lime curve, almost flat, is the step Muon takes from this momentum. Over all 48 hidden matrices of the 8 blocks, Muon's step has its largest singular value 1.3 to 1.5 times its median."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"All numbers come from one training run per optimizer with one seed."}),e.jsx(l,{title:"The measurement in numbers",children:e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The orange curve shows how the momentum's singular values fall: 0.41 at the second value, 0.20 at the tenth, then a long tail to a median of about 0.035. Over all 48 hidden matrices, Muon's momentum has its largest singular value 23 to 157 times the median in the attention matrices and 8 to 56 times in the MLP matrices. The single-step gradient is even more uneven, at 17 to 423 times. Momentum adds up the gradients of many steps, and its ratio is 1.3 to 3.3 times lower than one gradient's. So the imbalance is already present in each gradient, and averaging does not create it. These numbers were recorded after 4,000 of the configured 15,400 steps, after the 1,540-step warmup, so the learning rate is at its peak."})}),e.jsx(l,{title:"Why not count directions",children:e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The fewest singular values whose sum reaches 90% of the total sum is 352 for this momentum, against 423 for the Gaussian matrix, because 500 small values still add up. The ratio to the median and the energy share show what matters for a step: how much farther the step moves along the top direction than along a typical one."})})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"One global step size starves the quiet directions",accent:1,number:"15",items:"3",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
