`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Why a concentrated update needs more than one scale",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Jordan et al. report that the updates of SGD with momentum and of Adam for 2D parameters typically have a very high condition number, the ratio of the largest singular value to the smallest. The updates are almost low-rank matrices ",e.jsx(a,{id:"jordan2024muon",children:"Jordan et al., 2024"}),". Our AdamW run shows it too. Over the model's 48 hidden matrices, the largest-to-median ratio of AdamW's momentum is 8 to 93 times. AdamW's step is still 7 to 43 times uneven. Its division acts on one coordinate at a time, and a singular direction is spread over many coordinates, so the division cannot equalize directions."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Any optimizer that scales the whole update by one number, for example normalizing the update matrix by its norm, keeps the ratios between its singular values. The few directions with large singular values take almost all of the step, and the many directions with small singular values barely move."})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Muon equalizes the update's singular directions",accent:2,build:!0,number:"16",steps:"2",children:[e.jsx(s,{math:"M = U \\Sigma V^\\top \\qquad \\mathrm{msign}(M) = U V^\\top",label:"(4)"}),e.jsx(s,{math:"M_t = \\beta M_{t-1} + G_t \\qquad W_t = W_{t-1} - \\eta\\, \\mathrm{msign}(M_t)",label:"(4a)"}),e.jsxs("div",{className:"[main[data-lecture-mode=present]_&]:lg:grid [main[data-lecture-mode=present]_&]:items-start [main[data-lecture-mode=present]_&]:gap-6 [main[data-lecture-mode=present]_&]:lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,1fr)]",children:[e.jsx("div",{"data-layout-panel":"text",className:"min-w-0",children:e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
