`]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:'Why "Nesterov-style."'})," Plain momentum would orthogonalize ",e.jsx(s,{math:"M_t",display:!1})," itself. The blend uses ",e.jsx(s,{math:"G_t + \\beta M_t",display:!1})," instead. Substituting ",e.jsx(s,{math:"M_t = \\beta M_{t-1} + G_t",display:!1})," gives ",e.jsx(s,{math:"(1 + \\beta) G_t + \\beta^2 M_{t-1}",display:!1,trail:","})," so the newest gradient gets more weight than it would in ",e.jsx(s,{math:"M_t",display:!1,trail:"."})," This acts as a look-ahead toward the momentum of the next step, the same idea behind Nesterov momentum for SGD."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"What the course adds."})," The slide's code leaves out the tall-matrix transpose, which the blog's published implementation has. Your ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"Muon"})," keeps the transpose and adds three things to the blog's method: Moonlight's rescale from the previous slide, decoupled weight decay as in AdamW, and Newton–Schulz in fp32. One step on a hidden ",e.jsx(s,{math:"m \\times n",display:!1})," matrix is then"]}),e.jsx(s,{math:"W_t = (1-\\eta\\lambda)\\,W_{t-1} - \\eta \\cdot 0.2\\sqrt{\\max(m, n)}\\;\\mathrm{NS}_5(G_t + \\beta M_t),"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["where ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\mathrm{NS}_5",display:!1})," is five Newton–Schulz steps."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Four practices keep Muon stable at scale",accent:6,number:"20",items:"4",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
