`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"WSD starts the decay only when a finished model is needed",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["With cosine, a longer run means starting over, and WSD removes that coupling. ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Decay"})," is run as a branch: when you need a finished checkpoint, you take a stable checkpoint and decay a copy of it, while the original stable run continues at its constant rate. The stable run commits to no end point, and each decay leg sets its own horizon."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Kimi K2 used this schedule. After its warmup, it trained at a constant 2e-4 for 10T tokens. It then decayed along a cosine to 2e-5 over 5.5T tokens, which ended the 15.5T-token main run ",e.jsx(a,{id:"kimik2-2025",children:"Kimi Team, 2025"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The course schedule, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"wsd_with_warmup"}),", has the same three phases with a different decay. It warms up linearly over ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"warmup_steps"}),", holds the full rate up to and including step ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"decay_start_frac * max_steps"}),", then falls linearly to ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"min_lr_frac"})," times the peak at ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"max_steps"}),". The Week 5 configs set ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"min_lr_frac: 0"}),", so the course decays linearly to zero, where K2's decay is a cosine to 2e-5. The provided ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"cosine_with_warmup"})," also decays to zero, so the course's cosine and WSD runs share a floor."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Branch a decay leg without ending the stable run",accent:4,number:"30",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
