`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"What K2's report shows and leaves open",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Monitoring."})," QK-Clip needs each head's max logit at every step, so K2 measures it throughout the run, and a claim of zero loss spikes can only be checked against such traces. The course trainer logs ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"max_attn_logit"}),", the largest masked logit over all heads and layers, on one step axis with the loss and the other signals. The trainer's function ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"max_attention_logit"})," computes this logit in fp32 with autocast off. It runs on the first sequence of each logged step only, so the value samples the batch. K2's per-head clip, in contrast, covers every sequence."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Schedule."})," K2 trains at a constant global batch of 67M tokens ",e.jsx(a,{id:"kimik2-2025",children:"Kimi Team, 2025"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Precision."})," K2 stores the parameters in bf16 and their gradient-accumulation buffer in fp32 ",e.jsx(a,{id:"kimik2-2025",children:"Kimi Team, 2025"}),". The report gives no format for the optimizer state and does not mention an fp32 master copy of the weights ",e.jsx(a,{id:"kimik2-2025",children:"Kimi Team, 2025"}),". So it leaves open whether K2 keeps the safeguard that stops small updates from vanishing in bf16."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["What changed between the two runs? K2 attributes the difference to MuonClip, its name for the optimizer that combines Muon, weight decay, RMS matching and QK-Clip ",e.jsx(a,{id:"kimik2-2025",children:"Kimi Team, 2025"}),". In the main run, QK-Clip held each head's max logit ",e.jsx(s,{math:"S^h_{\\max}",display:!1})," at the threshold ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\tau",display:!1})," or below. The runs also differ in scale: the test run has 53B total parameters, the main run 1T. The report does not give the test run's batch size, schedule or precision. Those factors are uncontrolled, so the report cannot show that the clip alone made the difference. It does show that the main run kept in range the quantity the test run lost control of."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"We read the five choices as one training system, and K2's recipe as one way of setting all five."})]})]})}),`
`,e.jsxs(h,{tab:"Going further",children:[e.jsxs(i,{title:"Six more ideas from recent reports",accent:0,number:"48",children:[e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Technique"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Problem it addresses"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["Steepest descent under a norm ",e.jsx(a,{id:"bernstein2024norm",children:"Bernstein & Newhouse, 2024"})]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"why msign is the best step under the spectral norm"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["Logit controls compared across labs (",e.jsx(a,{id:"alephalpha2026kolibri",paren:!1,children:"Aleph Alpha, 2026"}),"; ",e.jsx(a,{id:"qwen2026qwen38flashnext",paren:!1,children:"Qwen Team, 2026"}),")"]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"which control keeps attention logits bounded, tested on proxy models"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["Batch-size warmup (",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekv3-2024",paren:!1,children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2024b"}),"; ",e.jsx(a,{id:"qwen2026qwen38flashnext",paren:!1,children:"Qwen Team, 2026"}),")"]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"whether ramping the batch early helps, and why reports disagree"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["Massive activations, sinks and logit growth ",e.jsx(a,{id:"sun2024massive",children:"Sun et al., 2024"})]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"three extreme-value reports with separate mechanisms"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["bf16 gradient sums (",e.jsx(a,{id:"nvidia2026nemotron3ultra",paren:!1,children:"NVIDIA, 2026"}),"; ",e.jsx(a,{id:"poolside2026laguna",paren:!1,children:"Poolside Team et al., 2026"}),")"]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"two 2026 runs that failed because a sum stayed in bf16"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["Four labs' full recipes: ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekai2026deepseekv4",paren:!1,children:"DeepSeek V4"}),", ",e.jsx(a,{id:"kimik2-2025",paren:!1,children:"Kimi K2"}),", ",e.jsx(a,{id:"olmo3-2025",paren:!1,children:"OLMo 3"}),", ",e.jsx(a,{id:"microsoftai2026maithinking1",paren:!1,children:"MAI-Thinking-1"})]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"how the five choices combine in one run"})]})]})]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Six topics beyond the lecture",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The slides after this map are for students who want to go further. Nothing in this tab is quizzed."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Bernstein and Newhouse frame an optimizer as steepest descent under a norm: msign is the best step under the spectral norm, and sign descent is the best step under the ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\ell_\\infty",display:!1})," norm ",e.jsx(a,{id:"bernstein2024norm",children:"Bernstein & Newhouse, 2024"}),". Labs keep attention logits bounded in different ways (QK-Clip, RMSNorm on queries and KV entries, Muon per head), and two labs compared these controls on small proxy models trained at high learning rates. Batch-size warmup grows the batch early in training, but 2026 reports disagree on whether it still helps. Massive activations, attention sinks and attention-logit growth are often mentioned together, yet they concern different tensors and have separate mechanisms ",e.jsx(a,{id:"sun2024massive",children:"Sun et al., 2024"}),". Two 2026 runs became unstable because a gradient sum stayed in bf16 (",e.jsx(a,{id:"nvidia2026nemotron3ultra",paren:!1,children:"NVIDIA, 2026"}),"; ",e.jsx(a,{id:"poolside2026laguna",paren:!1,children:"Poolside Team et al., 2026"}),"). Four labs' full recipes show how this lecture's five choices combine in a single run."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Steepest descent under a norm",optional:!0,accent:3,number:"49",items:"6",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
