`]}),e.jsx(f,{type:"key",children:e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Optimizers are choices of norm"})}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Steepest descent under three norms",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["To first order, a step ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\Delta",display:!1})," changes the loss by the Frobenius inner product ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\langle G, \\Delta \\rangle_F",display:!1})," of the gradient ",e.jsx(s,{math:"G",display:!1})," and the step. Steepest descent picks the step that lowers this most among all steps of a given size in the chosen norm."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Under the Frobenius norm the solution is normalized gradient descent: the step's singular values stay proportional to those of ",e.jsx(s,{math:"G",display:!1,trail:"."})," Under the coordinate ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\ell_\\infty",display:!1})," norm the solution is sign descent, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\Delta^* = -\\eta\\operatorname{sign}(G)",display:!1,trail:"."})," Adam reduces to sign descent only when its EMAs are switched off (",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\beta_1 = \\beta_2 = 0",display:!1,trail:")"})," and ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\epsilon",display:!1})," is small next to ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\lvert g_t\\rvert",display:!1,trail:"."})," Ordinary Adam, with its stored moments, is not sign descent. Under the spectral norm the solution is ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\Delta^* = -\\eta UV^\\top",display:!1,trail:","})," with ",e.jsx(s,{math:"U",display:!1})," and ",e.jsx(s,{math:"V",display:!1})," from the SVD of ",e.jsx(s,{math:"G",display:!1,trail:"."})," This is the msign that Muon computes with Newton–Schulz iterations, and every singular value of the step equals ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\eta",display:!1,trail:"."})," Shampoo, an optimizer with a left and a right matrix preconditioner, is also steepest descent under the spectral norm ",e.jsx(a,{id:"bernstein2024norm",children:"Bernstein & Newhouse, 2024"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A spectral-norm bound on ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\Delta",display:!1})," limits the largest change in how much a linear layer amplifies any input. A Frobenius bound does too, because ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\lVert \\Delta \\rVert_2 \\le \\lVert \\Delta \\rVert_F",display:!1,trail:"."})]}),e.jsx(l,{title:"The amplification bound",children:e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["For a linear layer ",e.jsx(s,{math:"y = Wx",display:!1,trail:","})," a weight change ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\Delta",display:!1})," changes the output by ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\Delta x",display:!1,trail:","})," with ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\lVert \\Delta x \\rVert_2 \\le \\lVert \\Delta \\rVert_2 \\lVert x \\rVert_2",display:!1,trail:"."})]})}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The norm view derives Muon's update, but it may not be why Muon trains well. Shumaylov et al. tested two modifications of Muon's update. One replaced the unit singular values with pseudo-random values between 0 and 1, the other with powers of the singular values that reverse their order. Neither modified update is steepest descent under any norm that depends only on the singular values. On a 124M-parameter GPT-2 both reached about Muon's validation loss, while SGD with only its largest singular values removed did not ",e.jsx(a,{id:"shumaylov2026muon",children:"Shumaylov et al., 2026"}),". The runs are small and include no comparison with AdamW, so they bear on why Muon works, not on how much it gains."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Logit controls across labs",optional:!0,accent:2,number:"50",items:"6",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
