`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Four 2026 reports split on batch warmup",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Batch-size warmup starts with a small batch and grows it, on the argument that the critical batch size is small early in training. DeepSeek V3 is the classic example: its ramp lets the run use smaller, efficient batches early and more parallelism later. The report does not test the ramp on its own ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekv3-2024",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2024b"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Qwen3.8-Flash-Next retested the ramp under Muon with two variants that use different peak learning rates. Both ended within run-to-run variance of the constant batch but slightly worse, needed 18.8% more optimizer steps, and changed no stability measure. Qwen concludes the warmup is no longer necessary and gives three reasons. Under Muon, a batch below the predicted optimum costs more than one above it. Muon keeps its data efficiency at larger batches, whereas AdamW's degrades. And a large batch gives every expert enough tokens ",e.jsx(a,{id:"qwen2026qwen38flashnext",children:"Qwen Team, 2026"}),"."]}),e.jsx(l,{title:"Qwen3.8's ramp test",children:e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The test used a mixture-of-experts model with 10.8B total and 0.89B active parameters, trained on 4T tokens. The ramp grew the batch from 6.3M to 25.2M tokens by 524B tokens, run once with the constant-batch run's peak learning rate and once with a lower one."})}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["DeepSeek ramped V4-Flash's batch to 75.5M tokens ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekai2026deepseekv4",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2026a"}),', then trained V4.1-Flash on 45T tokens at a fixed 100.6M-token batch "with no instability", without saying why it changed ',e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekai2026deepseekv41",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2026b"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Mistral Large 4, trained with AdamW, ramps its batch from 1× to 4× its predicted batch size. It argues that early on a gradient from a fraction of the batch suffices, so a large batch wastes compute and tokens, and that the ramp acts as a regularizer, more so the more often data repeats. It chose 1× to 4× for wall-clock time, because larger batches use the hardware more efficiently ",e.jsx(a,{id:"mistral2026large4",children:"Mistral AI, 2026"}),"."]}),e.jsx(l,{title:"Mistral's ramp experiments",children:e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A ramp from 0.25× to 2× its predicted batch size performed best in Mistral's experiments ",e.jsx(a,{id:"mistral2026large4",children:"Mistral AI, 2026"}),"."]})}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:['A.X K2, also trained with AdamW, ramps over roughly its first 295B tokens "to mitigate gradient spikes" ',e.jsx(a,{id:"baek2026axk2",children:"Baek et al., 2026"}),". Neither Mistral nor A.X K2 publishes numbers for its ramp. The one measured test ran under Muon, so treat the optimizer as one possible reason for the split, not a settled rule."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Massive activations, sinks and logit growth",optional:!0,accent:1,number:"52",items:"4",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
